{"name":"Blarney Stone","hex":"#027944","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Blarney Stone","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A stone set into the upper wall of Blarney Castle in Ireland, which if kissed is supposed to bestow the gift of the gab or blarney."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Blarney_Stone"}],"entries":[{"title":"Blarney Stone","blocks":[{"text":"The Blarney Stone is a block of Carboniferous limestone built into the battlements of Blarney Castle, Blarney, about 8 kilometres from the centre of Cork City, Ireland. According to legend, kissing the stone endows the kisser with the gift of the gab. The stone was set into a tower of the castle in 1446. The castle is a popular tourist site in Ireland, attracting visitors from all over the world to kiss the stone and tour the castle and its gardens."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/The_Blarney_Stone.jpg/330px-The_Blarney_Stone.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blarney_Stone","wikidata":"Q2369564"},{"title":"Blarney","blocks":[{"text":"Blarney is a suburban town within the administrative area of Cork City in Ireland. It is located approximately 8 kilometres (5 mi) north-west of the city centre. It is the site of Blarney Castle, home of the legendary Blarney Stone. Blarney is part of the Dáil constituency of Cork North-Central. It is surrounded by the suburban villages of Tower, Cloghroe and Kerry Pike, all on the outskirts of Cork City."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/BlarneyCastleView1.jpg/330px-BlarneyCastleView1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blarney","wikidata":"Q2133873"},{"title":"Rock (geology)","blocks":[{"text":"In geology, a rock is any naturally occurring solid mass or aggregate of minerals or mineraloid matter. It is categorized by the minerals included, its chemical composition, and the way in which it is formed. Rocks form the Earth's outer solid layer, the crust, and most of its interior, except for the liquid outer core and pockets of magma in the asthenosphere. The study of rocks involves multiple subdisciplines of geology, including petrology and mineralogy. It may be limited to rocks found on Earth, or it may include planetary geology that studies the rocks of other celestial objects."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Marsh_Butte_and_Geikie_Peak%2C_Grand_Canyon.jpg/330px-Marsh_Butte_and_Geikie_Peak%2C_Grand_Canyon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rock_(geology)","wikidata":"Q8063"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/The_Blarney_Stone.jpg/500px-The_Blarney_Stone.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/The_Blarney_Stone.jpg/1280px-The_Blarney_Stone.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/The_Blarney_Stone.jpg/500px-The_Blarney_Stone.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Blarney Stone","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThe_Blarney_Stone.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/Blarney_Stone_from_below.jpg/500px-Blarney_Stone_from_below.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/Blarney_Stone_from_below.jpg/1280px-Blarney_Stone_from_below.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/Blarney_Stone_from_below.jpg/500px-Blarney_Stone_from_below.jpg?utm_source=